# Retry design evidence

This is a synthetic, frozen project record. The projects and source identifiers below are
fictional fixtures, not real repositories or live claims.

## Request and constraints

Design retries for an internal Node.js client making idempotent GET requests. The selected
contract requires caller cancellation during backoff, a separate attempt budget per request
and the existing overall deadline. POST requests must never be retried automatically. Our
current client has no retry mechanism or installed retry dependency. The team has not implemented
cancellable backoff before. Recommend an approach and the checks needed before implementation;
dependency adoption and code copying have not been approved.

## Available evidence

The local client accepts an AbortSignal and passes it to its HTTP transport. The retained
platform API record documents an abortable timer with a signal option. This provides a primitive,
but does not settle attempt ownership or the retry loop. The following captured repository
excerpts are sufficient to compare those questions; external access is outside this task.

### RapidRetry, source snapshot rr-7

The retained README advertises 40,000 stars, daily commits and cancellation support. Its public
retry operation accepts `signal`; only the HTTP call consumes it:

```js
let attempts = 0;
export async function retry(operation, signal, limit) {
  while (attempts < limit) {
    attempts += 1;
    try { return await operation(signal); }
    catch (error) {
      if (!error.transient) { throw error; }
      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 1000));
    }
  }
  throw new Error('attempt limit');
}
```

The captured tests cover one successful retry and permanent errors, but not concurrent callers
or cancellation during backoff. Issue 18 reports that cancellation waits for the timer; issue
23 reports exhausted budgets leaking between requests. Neither issue has a recorded resolution
in rr-7. The capture contains no license file or verified upstream license declaration. A README
sentence says "use freely"; downstream compatibility has not been established.

### QuietRetry, source snapshot qr-3

The captured source creates its attempt counter inside each invocation and passes the caller's
signal to both the HTTP operation and its abortable timer. Its retry predicate is caller-supplied.
The repository's retained test report passes cancellation-during-backoff, concurrent-budget and
permanent-error tests. No test covers our client's overall deadline or HTTP method policy.
Issue 9 documents that applications must enforce an overall deadline separately.

This small stable project has had no commits for eight months. Its captured release note says
maintenance is limited to defects; the captured history includes a resolved cancellation defect.
The captured MIT license names the upstream authors and requires retaining its notice for copies.
No third-party runtime dependency is declared. Those records do not establish current upstream
status, complete license compatibility with this project or behavior in our client.
